## TLDR
This issue suggests removing usages of `event.keyCode` (since it's deprecated) in favor of `event.key`.  Optionally, this can be enforced from that point forward with tooling.

## Overview

Many are not aware that although all the latest versions of the browsers still support `KeyboardEvent.keyCode`, it's been deprecated: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/KeyboardEvent/keyCode

enter, `KeyboardEvent.key`: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/KeyboardEvent/key

This event method is the heir apparent for keyboard management.

### Is this a breaking change?

From my understanding of EUI's browser support level this should not be a breaking change.

Consider the following example from [`src/components/color_picker/saturation.tsx`](https://github.com/elastic/eui/blob/master/src/components/color_picker/saturation.tsx#L134) (of course, in practice a constant map could be used instead of `'ArrowDown'` literals, _although_ the literals can be type checked by TypeScript (thereby preventing typos) [although they aren't by default](https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/blob/master/types/react/index.d.ts#L1222)):

```diff
- switch (e.keyCode) {
+ switch (e.key) {
-  case keyCodes.DOWN:
+  case 'ArrowDown':
    e.preventDefault();
    newTop = top < height ? top + heightScale : height;
    break;
-  case keyCodes.LEFT:
+  case 'ArrowLeft':
    e.preventDefault();
    newLeft = left > 0 ? left - widthScale : 0;
    break;
-  case keyCodes.UP:
+  case 'ArrowUp':
    e.preventDefault();
    newTop = top > 0 ? top - heightScale : 0;
    break;
-  case keyCodes.RIGHT:
+  case 'ArrowRight':
    e.preventDefault();
    newLeft = left < width ? left + widthScale : width;
    break;
  default:
    return;
}
```

30ish files in the codebase use a [`keyCodes`](https://github.com/elastic/eui/blob/master/src/services/key_codes.ts) map.  Something similar could be trivially done to swap out the implementations for various places in the codebase.

I can verify that all of the constants in `event.keyCode`, at least, are covered by modern-day counterparts in `event.key` like `F2`, `Enter`, `Tab`, `ArrowDown`, etc..  Here's the official list of possible values: https://www.w3.org/TR/uievents-key/#named-key-attribute-values

## Preventing in the future

This can (_I think_) be prevented in the future by overlaying the types using [module augmentation](https://github.com/typescript-cheatsheets/react-typescript-cheatsheet/blob/master/docs/advanced/types-react-ap.md#adding-non-standard-attributes).
