If you use the `button` prop for an EuiPopover, everything works as expected. Clicking that passed in button node will open the popover, and clicking anywhere outside the popover content will close it (as will any other way of changing the `isOpen` property).

But if you have a separate button outside of the popover content that sets the `isOpen` prop to true, it will briefly open the popover, then close it. 

Example: https://codesandbox.io/s/1rm5o5yr94

You can get around this by having your open method stop the native event's propagation so that the click event doesn't continue to bubble through the other document listeners, which is what eventually closes the popover.

```js
open = e => {
  // thought "stopPropagation" might fix but it just makes
  // BOTH buttons have the weird autoclose behavior!
  // e.stopPropagation();

  // this works because react listens to all events at the
  // document level, and you need to interact with the native
  // event's propagation to short-circuit outside click handler
  // see also: https://stackoverflow.com/a/24421834
  e.nativeEvent.stopImmediatePropagation();

  this.setState({ isOpen: true });
};
```

The better fix would be in EUI. The reason this is happening is because the outside click handler component is _always_ mounted, so the listener is listening for clicks all the time and closing the popover on _every_ click it hears. So a click on a button that is outside the popover and that opens the popover will continue to bubble until it reaches the always-mounted outside click handler which will then close it.

One solution would be to only mount the outside click handler component when the popover is open, then unmount when it closes. But that would screw up the popover animations in and out. We probably need to either handle that animation using React's transition stuff, or modify the outside click handler so that it can mount and unmount the actual listener based on some passed in prop.

I'll try a basic PR with that 2nd option later and see what y'all think. :)

