When your `field_value_selection` filter has item-level `field` definitions, the `field_value_selection` filter still assumes a top level field is defined, so if _any filter query whatsoever_ is present, the filter button is marked active (even though no own filter options in its menu are active), provided `multiSelect` is not set to `or`

<img width="332" alt="Screen Shot 2019-04-09 at 21 19 23" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/934293/55843166-3bf31680-5b0d-11e9-98d1-3f77f99e6536.png">

Culprit:

```js
  isActiveField(field) {
    const { query } = this.props;
    const multiSelect = this.resolveMultiSelect();

    if (multiSelect === 'or') {
      return query.hasOrFieldClause(field);
    }

    return query.hasSimpleFieldClause(field);
  }
```

This is caused by `query.hasSimpleFieldClause(field)` receiving `field` as `undefined` for the top-level field, and `query.hasSimpleFieldClause(undefined)` is happy to return `true`. The ask would be that `isActiveField` short-circuits in the presence of an `undefined` field, since it makes no sense to have filters that target no field at all (or in any case to consider such a broken filter active). Since the assumption is that void `field` means all the options of `field_value_selection` define their own fields, all would be fine with the world if we did:

```diff
  isActiveField(field) {
    const { query } = this.props;
    const multiSelect = this.resolveMultiSelect();

+   if (field === undefined) {
+     return false
+   }
+
    if (multiSelect === 'or') {
      return query.hasOrFieldClause(field);
    }

    return query.hasSimpleFieldClause(field);
  }
```

In parallel, however, doing this means we wouldn't mark it as an active filter while the option list isn't displayed, presumably because when we close the options popover, the option list in state is killed with `this.setState({ popoverOpen: false, options: null })`

