|------|-------|-------|
| `src/plugins/bfetch/public/batching/create_streaming_batched_function.ts` | 116 | Unguarded `JSON.parse` in RxJS `next` handler |
| `src/plugins/bfetch/public/batching/create_streaming_batched_function.ts` | 119, 122 | No bounds check on `response.id` |
| `src/plugins/bfetch/public/streaming/split.ts` | 63-64 | Flushes partial buffer on completion |
| `src/plugins/opensearch_dashboards_utils/common/defer.ts` | 43-50 | `Defer` has no timeout — orphaned promises hang forever |

### Description

The PPL search strategy's aggregation loop in `ppl_search_strategy.ts` contains two bugs: (1) it mutates the shared `request` object in-place, corrupting the original query, and (2) it resets `dataFrame.aggs` to `{}` on every loop iteration, discarding previously collected aggregation results so only the last successful aggregation survives.

### Bug 1: Request mutation corrupts original query (line 67)

```typescript
// ppl_search_strategy.ts:67
request.body.query.query = aggQueryString;
```

This directly mutates `request.body.query.query`, which is the same object referenced by the `query` variable captured at line 41 (`const query: Query = request.body.query`). The user's original PPL query (e.g., `source = my_table`) is permanently overwritten with the aggregation query string. If any downstream code inspects `request.body.query.query` after this search strategy returns (logging, caching, retry logic), it sees the aggregation query instead.

### Bug 2: Aggregation results reset on each iteration (line 70)

```typescript
// ppl_search_strategy.ts:70 (inside the for loop)
(dataFrame as IDataFrameWithAggs).aggs = {};
```

This resets `aggs` to `{}` on **every iteration**. Line 71 then adds only the current key. If `aggConfig.qs` contains multiple entries, each iteration wipes out all previously stored aggregation results. Only the last key survives.

Currently masked because the client-side `getAggConfig()` at `ppl_search_interceptor.ts:172` typically produces a single `qs` entry. But the `QueryAggConfig` type and the server-side `Object.entries(aggConfig.qs)` loop clearly expect multiple entries.

### Existing test gap

The test at `ppl_search_strategy.test.ts:365` ("should handle aggConfig when response succeeds") only uses a single `qs` entry, so neither bug is detected.

### Suggested fix

Move `aggs = {}` before the loop, and shallow-copy the request per iteration instead of mutating:

```typescript
if (aggConfig) {
  (dataFrame as IDataFrameWithAggs).aggs = {};  // once, before loop
  for (const [key, aggQueryString] of Object.entries(aggConfig.qs)) {
    const aggRequest = {
      ...request,
      body: {
        ...request.body,
        query: { ...request.body.query, query: aggQueryString },
      },
    };
    const rawAggs: any = await pplFacet.describeQuery(context, aggRequest);
    if (!rawAggs.success) continue;
    (dataFrame as IDataFrameWithAggs).aggs[key] = rawAggs.data.datarows?.map(
      (hit: any) => ({ key: hit[1], value: hit[0] })
    );
  }
}
```

Also add a multi-key `qs` test case to catch regressions.

